6", "role": "support"}, {"trace_id": "step_07", "role": "confounder_control"}, {"trace_id": "step_08", "role": "confounder_control"}], "narrative": "No direct 1385->1186 edge exists (step_03, step_04). A static path does exist, 1385->639->528->1186 (and via 1688 or 1818), but 528 and 1818 are high-fan-in aggregators with 72 and 26 in-neighbours respectively (step_07, step_08), so this static reachability is a classic hub artefact rather than proof of influence. Both endpoints are very low-activity (11 and 9 events over the full 100k horizon, step_02) in a sparse, near-non-repeating graph (repeat ratio 0.053, step_01), so a three-hop chain with every consecutive gap <= 600 inside the window is a priori improbable. Decisively, none of the available tools expose event timestamps, so I could not verify the time-ordering or the 600-unit relay constraint for any candidate chain. I decline to certify a cascade on time-ignoring connectivity alone; the temporal evidence needed is simply not obtainable with the instrumentation used."}, "raw_final_response": "I have exhausted my tool budget. Here is my assessment.\n\n**What I established:**\n- The graph is large and sparse: 16,325 events, 15,458 distinct edges, repeat ratio 0.053 (step_01) \u2014 i.e., almost every pair interacts exactly once, so per-node activity is thin.\n- Node 1385 has only 11 events / 11 distinct partners, and 1186 only 9 events / 9 partners over the *entire* 100k horizon (step_02). Neither is a hub; both are very low-rate emitters.\n- 1385's out-neighbours are {379, 457, 595, 639, 675, 1409, 1688} (step_03); 1186's in-neighbours are only {528, 1259, 1818} (step_04). There is no direct 1385\u21921186 edge, so any cascade needs \u22652 relays.\n- The 2-hop neighbourhoods (step_05, step_06) do not meet: 1186's in-set is not reachable in one hop from 1385's out-set within those expansions.\n- Checking the two gateways into 1186: 528 has 72 in-neighbours including 639 and 1688 (step_07), and 1818 has 26 in-neighbours including 639 (step_08). So a *time-ignoring* path 1385\u2192639\u2192528\u21921186 (and variants via 1688/1818) does exist \u2014 but 528 and 1818 are high-fan-in aggregators, exactly the kind of node that manufactures spurious static paths.\n\n**Why I cannot call it \"present\":** every available tool aggregates over the whole horizon; none returns timestamps or per-edge times. I therefore have no evidence that the three required hops (1385\u2192639, 639\u2192528, 528\u21921186) occur in increasing time order inside [61549, 99948] with consecutive gaps \u2264 600. Given ~0.16 events/time-unit globally and only ~4 expected 1385-events in the window, a triple of hops each landing within a 600-unit relay budget is a priori unlikely, but \"unlikely\" is not the same as demonstrated-absent. The only positive evidence I hold is precisely the kind the task warns is insufficient: static connectivity through popular relays.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_20982933\",\n  \"decision\": \"insufficient\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"cascade\",\n  \"entities\": [1385, 1186, 639, 1688, 528, 1818],\n  \"window\": [61549, 99948],\n  \"confidence\": 0.5,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_01\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_02\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_03\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_04\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_05\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_06\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_07\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_08\", \"role\": \"confounder_control\"}\n  ],\n  \"narrative\": \"No direct 1385->1186 edge exists (step_03, step_04). A static path does exist, 1385->639->528->1186 (and via 1688 or 1818), but 528 and 1818 are high-fan-in aggregators with 72 and 26 in-neighbours respectively (step_07, step_08), so this static reachability is a classic hub artefact rather than proof of influence. Both endpoints are very low-activity (11 and 9 events over the full 100k horizon, step_02) in a sparse, near-non-repeating graph (repeat ratio 0.053, step_01), so a three-hop chain with every consecutive gap <= 600 inside the window is a priori improbable. Decisively, none of the available tools expose event timestamps, so I could not verify the time-ordering or the 600-unit relay constraint for any candidate chain. I decline to certify a cascade on time-ignoring connectivity alone; the temporal evidence needed is simply not obtainable with the instrumentation used.\"\n}\n```", "total_input_tokens": 21003, "total_output_tokens": 4556, "total_tokens": 25559, "total_latency_ms": 64143, "llm_calls": 5, "schema_repairs": 0, "status": "success", "error": ""}